{
  "text": " Do it again, said Innes. Callie stood up. She put her back foot behind her and dug it in, and dropped her weight, and set herself the way you set yourself for a sack coming off a hook. She did not lean. That was the whole difference, and she felt it the moment she did it. Leaning was going out toward something. This was not going anywhere. This was being the thing that stayed where it was. Innes threw a stone at her. It stopped a finger's width off her coat, and dropped. The shine was on her for about the space of one breath. It was a gray, faint sheen in the last of the light, like the surface of old glass. Then her foot slid a quarter of an inch in the gravel, and the whole thing went off her like water off a tipped plate, and she was only standing there again. Nobody said anything. From up on the wagon bed where she had been lying under a blanket with her eyes shut, Callie's mother said, Your grandfather used to say you could stand a sack of meal on your back foot all day, if you'd only stop arguing with it. Callie turned round. I know, she said. I remember. Above them the scarp went up into the dusk, a mile of pale road in a long, straight groove to the sky, with nothing on it anywhere that a person could have hooked a block to. Read the",
